# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""/procedimientos/parpados-sin-cicatrices — Spanish copy.

This page's job is to talk a reader OUT of the operation more often than into it,
and three statements do that work.

  "La operación más pequeña tiene un techo."
  It removes fat and tightens skin; it does not remove skin. If what you have is
  genuine excess, this improves it slightly and disappoints you. Establish which
  column you are in before anything else.

  "Está comprobando si usted necesita la otra operación."
  Almost everyone asking about this one wants it to be the answer — shorter,
  cheaper, awake, nothing to see. The consultation is mostly him testing whether
  it will do enough. Being told no here is the version that saves money.

  "Pregunte qué le hace el láser a SU piel."
  This is a Miami practice and his patients span a wide range of complexions.
  Resurfacing does not behave the same way on all of them. The page tells the
  reader to ask a specific question rather than offering a reassurance, which is
  the right shape for it and must not be turned into one.

"Sin cicatrices" is a claim about the outside of the face and the page says so
explicitly. Do not let it become "sin incisión" anywhere — there is an incision,
it is on the inside of the lid.

The comparison table is the spine of the page. Both columns keep their real
names: "Blefaroplastia" and "Sin cicatrices · CO2".
"""

